{
  "problem": "sum-of-three-triangular-numbers",
  "claim": "Prove that every nonnegative integer n can be written as a sum of three triangular numbers: there exist nonnegative integers a, b, c with n = T_a + T_b + T_c, where T_k = k(k+1)/2.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "claim": "Let n be an arbitrary nonnegative integer and set m = 8n + 3. Then m is a positive integer (m >= 3).",
      "justification": "given",
      "depends_on": [],
      "obligations": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "claim": "m = 8n + 3 satisfies m ≡ 3 (mod 8); in particular m is odd (8n is even, plus 3 is odd).",
      "justification": "congruence",
      "depends_on": ["s1"],
      "obligations": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "claim": "If m = 4^a(8b+7) for nonnegative integers a, b, then a = 0: for a >= 1 we would have 4 | 4^a(8b+7) = m, so m even, contradicting that m is odd.",
      "justification": "divisibility",
      "depends_on": ["s2"],
      "obligations": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "claim": "Hence m = 8n + 3 is NOT of the form 4^a(8b+7): the only possible exponent is a = 0 (by s3), but then m = 8b+7 ≡ 7 (mod 8), contradicting m ≡ 3 (mod 8) from s2.",
      "justification": "congruence",
      "depends_on": ["s2", "s3"],
      "obligations": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "claim": "Gauss–Legendre three-squares theorem (admitted as a citable input for this problem, not re-derived): a positive integer m is a sum of three integer squares if and only if m is not of the form 4^a(8b+7) with a, b nonnegative integers.",
      "justification": "lemma",
      "depends_on": [],
      "obligations": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s6",
      "claim": "Applying the admitted theorem (s5) to the positive integer m = 8n+3 (s1), which is not of the form 4^a(8b+7) (s4), yields integers x, y, z with x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 8n + 3.",
      "justification": "lemma",
      "depends_on": ["s1", "s4", "s5"],
      "obligations": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s7",
      "claim": "For every integer t, t^2 ≡ 0, 1, 4, 1 (mod 8) according as t ≡ 0, 1, 2, 3 (mod 4); in particular t^2 ≡ 1 (mod 8) exactly when t is odd, and t^2 ∈ {0, 4} (mod 8) when t is even.",
      "justification": "quadratic_residue",
      "depends_on": [],
      "obligations": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s8",
      "claim": "Case-splitting x, y, z modulo 4 and reducing each square mod 8 by s7, the congruence x^2 + y^2 + z^2 ≡ 3 (mod 8) (from s6 and s2) is satisfied only when each of x, y, z is odd; every combination with at least one even term gives a residue in {0,1,2,4,5,6} ≠ 3 (mod 8). Therefore x, y, z are all odd.",
      "justification": "case_split",
      "depends_on": ["s2", "s6", "s7"],
      "obligations": {
        "case_cover": { "modulus": 4, "residues": [0, 1, 2, 3] }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s9",
      "claim": "Replacing x, y, z by |x|, |y|, |z| does not change their squares (so s6 still holds), and since x, y, z are odd (s8) each of |x|, |y|, |z| is a positive odd integer. Write |x| = 2a+1, |y| = 2b+1, |z| = 2c+1 with a = (|x|-1)/2, b = (|y|-1)/2, c = (|z|-1)/2, which are nonnegative integers.",
      "justification": "algebra",
      "depends_on": ["s8"],
      "obligations": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s10",
      "claim": "For any nonnegative integer a, (2a+1)^2 = 4a^2 + 4a + 1 = 8·(a(a+1)/2) + 1 = 8·T_a + 1, and likewise for b and c. Summing gives |x|^2 + |y|^2 + |z|^2 = 8(T_a + T_b + T_c) + 3.",
      "justification": "algebra",
      "depends_on": ["s9"],
      "obligations": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s11",
      "claim": "Since |x|^2 + |y|^2 + |z|^2 = x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 8n + 3 (s6) and also equals 8(T_a + T_b + T_c) + 3 (s10), we get 8(T_a + T_b + T_c) + 3 = 8n + 3, hence T_a + T_b + T_c = n with a, b, c nonnegative integers.",
      "justification": "algebra",
      "depends_on": ["s1", "s6", "s10"],
      "obligations": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s12",
      "claim": "Prove that every nonnegative integer n can be written as a sum of three triangular numbers: there exist nonnegative integers a, b, c with n = T_a + T_b + T_c, where T_k = k(k+1)/2.",
      "justification": "conclusion",
      "depends_on": ["s11"],
      "obligations": {}
    }
  ]
}
